        "type": "thinking",
        "thinking": "Cloning per texel would be too slow for millions of points, so I should avoid that approach entirely. Instead, since the mantle centerline sits in the YZ plane, I can precompute a lookup mapping height y to tangent direction, then interpolate to get the tangent at any given y since y increases monotonically along the mantle.\n\nFrom that tangent I can derive a dorsal vector pointing outward (front) and its negation for the back, with sideways directions along X. This lets me compute each surface normal as a combination of the dorsal vector and the X axis based on angle φ, giving an accurate point on the tube's circular cross-section for placing glow lines, and I can reuse the same y-to-tangent table for the head sphere since it sits within the same range, clamping at the top where the tangent becomes vertical.\n\nFor the head's coloring, I'm weighing whether to give it its own \"up\" vector or keep it consistent with the mantle's dorsal-back scheme — the latter keeps head and mantle colors continuous at the junction (cyan belly facing front, flank-blue on top and sides), which reads oddly but is coherent, whereas a separate head-up vector would make the head's front match the flank and create a visible color mismatch with the mantle at the seam. I'll lean toward the mantle-consistent approach for continuity, accepting a lighter top-of-head, and move on to finalizing the belly/flank/back palette (pale cyan, blue flanks, navy back).\n\nNow I'm adding orange accent lines along the belly edges and a chevron ribbing pattern using a periodic modulo function on position to create subtle V-shaped stripes in deeper cyan across the belly. I'm also setting up a lookup table by sampling the catmull-rom path at 64 points and building a monotonic y-to-t search, then wiring that into the chain-building code.\n\nWriting mantle sweep code...\n\nThe catmull path stays smooth, so that's fine. Checking the head paint, it reuses the mantle's color function clamped by y, and since both overlap in the 1.55-2.83 range using the same function, colors stay continuous across the junction. To give the head more character I'll add dedicated markings through geometry—brow, horns, bezel, gunmetal accents.\n\nFor tentacles, I'm using a simple seam-based coloring by the first UV coordinate.\n\nNow I'm working out where the collar sits: the head top at y=2.83 corresponds to a mantle parameter around t=0.36, with a collar radius near 1.1-1.18. Tracing through the catmull segments at that t value, the resulting y position (~2.78) lands right at the front of the head, meaning the collar ring would partially intersect the head's front region rather than sitting cleanly below it.\n\nChecking the geometry more carefully, at t=0.4 (y=3.0) the head's front surface is already sunk below the mantle's ring radius, so placing the collar there works cleanly as the first rib — settling on collar t=0.40, with additional ribs at t=0.52, 0.63, 0.73, 0.82 shrinking radius from 1.1 down to 0.6, and starting the dorsal blades around t=0.30 extending to 0.93.\n\nNow I'm working out seven dorsal blade placements at t=0.3, 0.38, 0.46, 0.54, 0.62, 0.70, 0.78, with fins at 0.84 — checking that the first blade near the head top lands on the back/dorsal side where the head doesn't intrude. For each blade I'm computing the local frame from the mantle tangent at that point, deriving a perpendicular vector to orient the blade's cross-section, and sketching a simple convex outline that tapers from a narrow base to a sharp tip, leaning back toward the tail along the tangent direction.\n\nWorking out a non-self-intersecting fin outline with a slight concave notch at the back edge, apex displaced toward the tip for a shark-fin lean, keeping all vertices sharp since smoothing defaults to zero. Setting thickness to 0.07 and bevel to 0.015.\n\nFor colouring the blade, I want to paint based on vertical position relative to the hub radius so the upper portion reads as an orange